00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,500
This is a course about justice

4
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:37,000
and we begin with a story.

5
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,500
Suppose you're the driver of a trolley car and your trolley

6
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:44,000
car is hurtling down the track at 60 miles an hour.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,500
And at the end of the track

8
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,000
you notice five workers working on the track.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:51,000
You try to stop but

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
you can't. Your brakes don't work.

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
You feel desperate because you know that

12
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
if you crash into these five workers

13
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:00,000
they will all die.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Let's assume you know that for sure.

15
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,500
And so you feel helpless until you notice that

16
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:12,000
there is off to the right a side track.

17
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
And at the end of that track

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
there's one worker working on the track.

19
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Your steering wheel works so you can turn the

20
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:27,000
trolley car if you want to onto the side track

21
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
killing the one.

22
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,000
And you can't.

23
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
But sparing the five.

24
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Here's our first question.

25
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
What's the right thing to do?

26
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
What would you do?

27
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Let's take a poll.

28
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
How many would turn the

29
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
trolley car onto the side track?

30
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Raise your hands.

31
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:53,000
How many wouldn't?

32
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
How many would go straight ahead?

33
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Keep your hands up.

34
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Those of you who would go straight ahead.

35
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:05,000
A handful of people would.

36
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,000
The vast majority would turn.

37
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Let's hear first.

38
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Now we need to begin to investigate the reasons

39
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
why you think it's the right thing to do.

40
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Let's begin with those in the majority.

41
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Who would turn to go onto the side track?

42
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Why would you do it?

43
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
What would be your reason?

44
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Who's willing to volunteer a reason?

45
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Go ahead.

46
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Stand up.

47
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,500
Because it can't be right to kill five

48
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:38,000
people when you can only kill one person instead.

49
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,500
It wouldn't be right to kill five

50
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:47,000
if you could kill one person instead.

51
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,000
That's a good reason.

52
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:53,000
That's a good reason.

53
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Who else?

54
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
Does everybody agree with that reason?

55
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Go ahead.

56
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Well, I was thinking it was the same reason on 9-11 we regard the people who flew the plane into the

57
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Pennsylvania field as heroes because they chose to kill the people in the plane and not kill more people in big buildings.

58
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
So the principle there was the same on 9-11.

59
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,000
It's a tragic circumstance.

60
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
But better to kill one so that five can live?

61
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:26,500
Is that the reason most of you

62
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:28,000
had those of you who would turn?

63
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Yes?

64
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,500
Let's hear now from those in

65
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:39,000
the minority, those who wouldn't turn.

66
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Yes?

67
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,500
Well, I think that's the same type

68
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:45,000
of mentality that justifies genocide and totalitarianism.

69
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,500
In order to save one type

70
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:50,000
of race, you wipe out the other.

71
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
So what would you do in this case?

72
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:53,000
You would...

73
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:54,000
To avoid...

74
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
To avoid the horrors of genocide, you

75
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,000
would crash into the five and kill them?

76
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Presumably, yes.

77
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:06,000
You would...

78
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Yeah.

79
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Okay. Who else?

80
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
That's a brave answer. Thank you.

81
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Let's consider another trolley car case.

82
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
And see whether...

83
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Those of you in the majority

84
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
want to adhere to the principle,

85
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
better that one should die so that five should live.

86
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
This time you're not the driver

87
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,000
of the trolley car, you're an onlooker.

88
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,000
You're standing on a bridge overlooking a trolley car track.

89
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,000
And down the track comes a trolley car.

90
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
At the end of the track are five workers.

91
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,000
The brakes don't work.

92
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,000
The trolley car is about to

93
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
careen into the five and kill them.

94
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
And now, you're not the driver.

95
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,000
You really feel helpless.

96
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,500
Until you notice, standing next to you, leaning

97
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:11,000
over the bridge, is a very fat man.

98
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:20,000
And you could give him a shove.

99
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:26,000
He would fall over the bridge, onto the

100
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:31,000
track, right in the way of the trolley car.

101
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:35,000
He would die, but he would spare the five.

102
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,500
Now, how many would push

103
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:42,000
the fat man over the bridge?

104
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
Raise your hand.

105
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,000
How many wouldn't?

106
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Most of you would.

107
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Most of you would.

108
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
Most people wouldn't.

109
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Here's the obvious question.

110
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,000
What became of the principle, better to save

111
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,000
five lives even if it means sacrificing one,

112
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
what became of the principle that

113
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
almost everyone endorsed in the first case?

114
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
I need to hear from someone who

115
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
was in the majority in both cases.

116
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
How do you explain the difference between the two?

117
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:17,000
Yes.

118
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:18,000
The second one, I guess, involves

119
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,000
an active choice of, you know,

120
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:24,500
pushing the person down, which, I guess, that person himself

121
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:30,000
would otherwise not have been involved in the situation at all.

122
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:35,000
And so to choose on his behalf, I guess, to involve

123
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:40,000
him in something that he otherwise would have escaped is, I guess,

124
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,500
more than what you have in the first case, where

125
00:06:43,500 --> 00:06:47,000
the three parties, the driver and the two sets of workers,

126
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
are already, I guess, in the situation.

127
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
But the guy working, the one

128
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
on the track, off to the side,

129
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,500
he didn't choose to sacrifice his life any

130
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:59,000
more than the fat man did, did he?

131
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:04,000
That's true, but he was on the tracks.

132
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
This guy was on the bridge.

133
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:11,000
Go ahead.

134
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,000
You can come back if you want.

135
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:14,000
All right.

136
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:15,000
It's a hard question.

137
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:16,000
All right.

138
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:17,000
You did very well.

139
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
It's a hard question.

140
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:24,000
Who else can find a way of reconciling

141
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:29,000
the reaction of the majority in these two cases?

142
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:30,000
Yes.

143
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,500
Well, I guess in the first case, where

144
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:35,000
you have the one worker and the five,

145
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
it's choice between those two, and

146
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
you have to make a certain choice,

147
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:40,000
and people are going to

148
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:41,000
die because of the trolley car,

149
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
not necessarily because of your direct actions.

150
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
The trolley car is a runway thing,

151
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,000
and you're making a split-second choice,

152
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
whereas pushing the fat man over is

153
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
an actual act of murder on your part.

154
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,500
You have control over that, whereas you

155
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:56,000
may not have control over the trolley car.

156
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,000
So I think it's a slightly different situation.

157
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:00,000
All right.

158
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:01,000
Who has a reply?

159
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
No, that's good.

160
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:04,000
Who has a way?

161
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:05,000
Who wants to reply?

162
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,000
Is that a way out of this?

163
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
I don't think that's a very

164
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,000
good reason, because you choose to...

165
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
Either way, you have to choose who dies,

166
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,000
because you either choose to turn and kill the person,

167
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:19,000
which is an act of conscious thought to turn,

168
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
or you choose to push the fat man over,

169
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:24,000
which is also an act of conscious action.

170
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,000
So either way, you're making a choice.

171
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
Do you want to reply?

172
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,000
Well, I'm not really sure that that's the case.

173
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:32,000
It just still seems kind of different,

174
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,500
the act of actually pushing someone

175
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:37,000
over onto the tracks and killing him.

176
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
You are actually killing him yourself.

177
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
You're pushing him with your own hands.

178
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:42,000
You're pushing him.

179
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,000
And that's different than stealing.

180
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,000
You're steering something that is

181
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:48,000
going to cause death into another...

182
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:51,000
You know, it doesn't really sound right saying it now.

183
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:52,000
No, no, it's good.

184
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:53,000
But I'm up here.

185
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:54,000
It's good.

186
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:55,000
What's your name?

187
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:56,000
Andrew.

188
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:57,000
Andrew.

189
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:58,000
Let me ask you this question, Andrew.

190
00:08:58,000 --> 00:08:59,000
Yes.

191
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:05,000
Suppose, standing on the bridge next to the fat man,

192
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:06,000
I didn't have to push him.

193
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:07,500
Suppose he were standing over a

194
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:09,000
trap door that I could open

195
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
by turning a steering wheel like that.

196
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Would you turn?

197
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:22,000
For some reason, that still just seems more wrong.

198
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:23,000
Right?

199
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:24,500
I mean, maybe if you accidentally

200
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:26,000
like leaned into the steering wheel

201
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,000
or something like that.

202
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,000
But, or say that the car is hurtling

203
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,000
towards a switch that will drop the trap.

204
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
Then I could agree with that.

205
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:39,000
Fair enough.

206
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
It still seems wrong in a way

207
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
that it doesn't seem wrong in a way

208
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:44,500
that it doesn't seem wrong in

209
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:46,000
the first case to turn, you say.

210
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,000
And in another way, I mean, in the first situation,

211
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
you're involved directly with the situation.

212
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
In the second one, you're an onlooker as well.

213
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:53,000
All right.

214
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:54,500
So you have the choice of becoming

215
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:56,000
involved or not by pushing the fat man.

216
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,000
Let's forget for the moment about this case.

217
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
That's good.

218
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
Let's imagine a different case.

219
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:06,000
This time you're a doctor in an emergency room.

220
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:11,000
And six patients come to you.

221
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
They've been in a terrible trouble.

222
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
They're in a trolley car wreck.

223
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Five of them sustained moderate injuries.

224
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
One is severely injured.

225
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,500
You could spend all day caring

226
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:27,000
for the one severely injured victim.

227
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
But in that time, the five would die.

228
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:30,500
Or you could look after

229
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:32,000
the five, restore them to health,

230
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
but during that time, the

231
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
one severely injured person would die.

232
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,000
How many would save the five?

233
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
Now is the doctor.

234
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
How many would save the one?

235
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
Very few people.

236
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
Just a handful of people.

237
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
Same reason, I assume.

238
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:55,000
One life versus five?

239
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Now consider another doctor case.

240
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,000
This time you're a transplant surgeon.

241
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:06,000
