dies first and using their four healthy organs

278
00:12:45,086 --> 00:12:46,086
to save the other four.

279
00:12:49,586 --> 00:12:50,746
That's a pretty good idea.

280
00:12:52,306 --> 00:12:54,106
That's a great idea.

281
00:12:54,106 --> 00:12:56,206
Except for the fact that

282
00:12:56,206 --> 00:12:58,306
you just wrecked the philosophical point.

283
00:13:00,306 --> 00:13:05,306
Well, let's step back from these stories and these arguments

284
00:13:05,306 --> 00:13:08,626
to notice a couple of things about the way

285
00:13:08,626 --> 00:13:12,066
the arguments have begun to unfold.

286
00:13:12,066 --> 00:13:17,546
Certain moral principles have already begun to emerge

287
00:13:17,546 --> 00:13:20,626
in the history of the world.

288
00:13:20,626 --> 00:13:21,186
And they are.

289
00:13:21,186 --> 00:13:21,686
They are.

290
00:13:21,686 --> 00:13:22,186
They are.

291
00:13:22,186 --> 00:13:26,886
We have learned from the discussions we've had.

292
00:13:26,886 --> 00:13:32,186
And let's consider what those moral principles look like.

293
00:13:32,186 --> 00:13:35,806
The first moral principle that emerged in the discussion

294
00:13:35,806 --> 00:13:37,746
said the right thing to

295
00:13:37,746 --> 00:13:39,686
do, the moral thing to do,

296
00:13:39,686 --> 00:13:43,106
depends on the consequences that

297
00:13:43,106 --> 00:13:46,526
will result from your action.

298
00:13:46,526 --> 00:13:48,256
At the end of the

299
00:13:48,256 --> 00:13:49,986
day, better that five should live,

300
00:13:49,986 --> 00:13:50,505
even if one must die.

301
00:13:50,505 --> 00:13:51,186
Mm-hmm.

302
00:13:51,186 --> 00:13:51,846
That's right.

303
00:13:51,846 --> 00:13:52,086
All right.

304
00:13:52,086 --> 00:13:57,086
That's an example of consequentialist moral reasoning.

305
00:13:58,846 --> 00:14:01,486
Consequentialist moral reasoning locates morality

306
00:14:01,486 --> 00:14:02,936
in the consequences of an act,

307
00:14:02,936 --> 00:14:04,386
in the state of the world

308
00:14:04,386 --> 00:14:07,306
that will result from the thing you do.

309
00:14:08,766 --> 00:14:10,146
But then we went a little further,

310
00:14:10,146 --> 00:14:12,686
we considered those other cases,

311
00:14:12,686 --> 00:14:14,966
and people weren't so sure

312
00:14:14,966 --> 00:14:18,586
about consequentialist moral reasoning.

313
00:14:18,586 --> 00:14:20,666
When people hesitated to push

314
00:14:20,666 --> 00:14:22,746
the fat man over the bridge

315
00:14:25,886 --> 00:14:29,666
or to yank out the organs of the innocent patient,

316
00:14:29,666 --> 00:14:33,246
people gestured toward reasons having to do

317
00:14:34,906 --> 00:14:38,906
with the intrinsic quality of the act itself,

318
00:14:40,166 --> 00:14:42,726
consequences be what they may.

319
00:14:42,726 --> 00:14:45,166
People were reluctant.

320
00:14:45,166 --> 00:14:48,486
People thought it was just wrong, categorically wrong.

321
00:14:48,586 --> 00:14:53,266
To kill a person, an innocent person,

322
00:14:53,266 --> 00:14:56,446
even for the sake of saving five lives.

323
00:14:56,446 --> 00:14:58,246
At least people thought that

324
00:14:58,246 --> 00:15:03,246
in the second version of each story we considered.

325
00:15:03,866 --> 00:15:08,866
So this points to a second categorical way

326
00:15:12,546 --> 00:15:15,806
of thinking about moral reasoning.

327
00:15:15,806 --> 00:15:17,706
Categorical moral reasoning locates morality,

328
00:15:17,706 --> 00:15:17,946
categorical moral reasoning locates morality,

329
00:15:17,946 --> 00:15:18,026
categorical moral reasoning locates morality,

330
00:15:18,026 --> 00:15:18,166
categorical moral reasoning locates morality,

331
00:15:18,166 --> 00:15:21,226
and certain absolute moral requirements,

332
00:15:21,226 --> 00:15:23,805
certain categorical duties and rights,

333
00:15:23,805 --> 00:15:27,246
regardless of the consequences.

334
00:15:27,246 --> 00:15:28,906
We're going to explore in

335
00:15:28,906 --> 00:15:30,566
the days and weeks to come

336
00:15:30,566 --> 00:15:33,346
the contrast between consequentialist

337
00:15:33,346 --> 00:15:36,446
and categorical moral principles.

338
00:15:36,446 --> 00:15:39,086
The most influential example

339
00:15:39,086 --> 00:15:42,746
of consequential moral reasoning is utilitarianism,

340
00:15:42,746 --> 00:15:46,605
a doctrine invented by Jeremy Bentham,

341
00:15:46,605 --> 00:15:47,526
the 18th century English political scholar,

342
00:15:47,526 --> 00:15:50,526
and the most influential English political philosopher.

343
00:15:50,526 --> 00:15:53,026
The most important philosopher

344
00:15:53,026 --> 00:15:55,526
of categorical moral reasoning

345
00:15:56,666 --> 00:16:01,666
is the 18th century German philosopher Immanuel Kant.

346
00:16:02,426 --> 00:16:05,266
So we will look at those two different modes

347
00:16:05,266 --> 00:16:10,266
of moral reasoning, assess them, and also consider others.

348
00:16:10,626 --> 00:16:11,745
If you look at the

349
00:16:11,745 --> 00:16:12,865
syllabus, you'll notice that we read

350
00:16:12,865 --> 00:16:15,846
a number of great and famous books.

351
00:16:15,846 --> 00:16:17,366
Books by Aristotle.

352
00:16:17,526 --> 00:16:22,526
John Locke, Immanuel Kant, John Stuart Mill, and others.

353
00:16:22,526 --> 00:16:24,926
You'll notice too from the syllabus

354
00:16:24,926 --> 00:16:27,326
that we don't only read these books.

355
00:16:27,326 --> 00:16:30,706
We also take up

356
00:16:30,706 --> 00:16:34,086
contemporary political and legal controversies

357
00:16:34,086 --> 00:16:36,886
that raise philosophical questions.

358
00:16:36,886 --> 00:16:41,326
We will debate equality and inequality, affirmative action,

359
00:16:41,326 --> 00:16:43,956
free speech versus hate

360
00:16:43,956 --> 00:16:46,586
speech, same-sex marriage, military conscription,

361
00:16:46,586 --> 00:16:47,056
a range of practices that are not only

362
00:16:47,056 --> 00:16:47,526
in the books, but also in the books.

363
00:16:47,526 --> 00:16:49,466
We will also look at the philosophical questions.

364
00:16:49,466 --> 00:16:50,686
Why?

365
00:16:50,686 --> 00:16:55,226
Not just to enliven these abstract and distant books,

366
00:16:55,226 --> 00:16:58,146
but to make clear, to bring out what's at stake

367
00:16:58,146 --> 00:17:01,406
in our everyday lives, including our political lives,

368
00:17:03,226 --> 00:17:04,306
for philosophy.

369
00:17:05,366 --> 00:17:07,586
And so we will read these books,

370
00:17:07,586 --> 00:17:10,026
and we will debate these issues,

371
00:17:10,026 --> 00:17:13,886
and we'll see how each informs and illuminates the other.

372
00:17:15,326 --> 00:17:17,526
This may sound appealing enough,

373
00:17:17,526 --> 00:17:22,526
but here I have to issue a warning.

374
00:17:22,526 --> 00:17:25,226
And the warning is this.

375
00:17:25,226 --> 00:17:31,566
To read these books in this way,

376
00:17:31,566 --> 00:17:34,126
as an exercise in self-knowledge,

377
00:17:34,126 --> 00:17:38,566
to read them in this way carries certain risks.

378
00:17:38,566 --> 00:17:42,146
Risks that are both personal and political.

379
00:17:42,146 --> 00:17:47,526
Risks that every student of political philosophy has known.

380
00:17:47,526 --> 00:17:50,526
These risks spring from the fact

381
00:17:50,526 --> 00:17:55,526
that philosophy teaches us and unsettles us

382
00:17:55,526 --> 00:17:59,526
by confronting us with what we already know.

383
00:17:59,526 --> 00:18:02,526
There's an irony.

384
00:18:02,526 --> 00:18:06,026
The difficulty of this course consists in the fact

385
00:18:06,026 --> 00:18:08,526
that it teaches what you already know.

386
00:18:08,526 --> 00:18:12,026
It works by taking what

387
00:18:12,026 --> 00:18:15,526
we know from familiar unquestioned settings

388
00:18:15,526 --> 00:18:17,426
and making it strange.

389
00:18:17,526 --> 00:18:22,526
That's how those examples worked.

390
00:18:22,526 --> 00:18:25,526
The hypotheticals with which we began,

391
00:18:25,526 --> 00:18:28,526
with their mix of playfulness and sobriety.

392
00:18:28,526 --> 00:18:31,526
It's also how these philosophical books work.

393
00:18:31,526 --> 00:18:36,526
Philosophy estranges us from the familiar.

394
00:18:36,526 --> 00:18:39,526
Not by supplying new information,

395
00:18:39,526 --> 00:18:44,526
but by inviting and provoking a new way of seeing.

396
00:18:44,526 --> 00:18:45,526
But,

397
00:18:45,526 --> 00:18:46,526
but,

398
00:18:46,526 --> 00:18:49,526
and here's the risk.

399
00:18:49,526 --> 00:18:53,526
Once the familiar turns strange,

400
00:18:53,526 --> 00:18:56,526
it's never quite the same again.

401
00:18:56,526 --> 00:19:02,526
Self-knowledge is like lost innocence.

402
00:19:02,526 --> 00:19:05,526
However unsettling you find it,

403
00:19:05,526 --> 00:19:12,526
it can never be unthought or unknown.

404
00:19:12,526 --> 00:19:15,526
What makes this enterprise difficult,

405
00:19:15,526 --> 00:19:18,526
but also riveting,

406
00:19:18,526 --> 00:19:23,526
is that moral and political philosophy is a story.

407
00:19:23,526 --> 00:19:26,526
And you don't know where the story will lead,

408
00:19:26,526 --> 00:19:29,526
but what you do know is

409
00:19:29,526 --> 00:19:32,526
that the story is about you.

410
00:19:32,526 --> 00:19:35,526
Those are the personal risks.

411
00:19:35,526 --> 00:19:38,526
Now what of the political risks?

412
00:19:38,526 --> 00:19:42,526
One way of introducing a course like this

413
00:19:42,526 --> 00:19:43,526
would be to promise you

414
00:19:43,526 --> 00:19:44,526
that by reading these books,

415
00:19:44,526 --> 00:19:46,526
and debating these issues,

416
00:19:46,526 --> 00:19:47,526
you will become a better,

417
00:19:47,526 --> 00:19:49,526
more responsible citizen.

418
00:19:49,526 --> 00:19:52,526
You will examine the presuppositions of public policy.

419
00:19:52,526 --> 00:19:55,526
You will hone your political judgment.

420
00:19:55,526 --> 00:19:57,526
You will become a more

421
00:19:57,526 --> 00:19:59,526
effective participant in public affairs.

422
00:19:59,526 --> 00:20:04,526
But this would be a partial and misleading promise.

423
00:20:04,526 --> 00:20:05,526
Political philosophy,

424
00:20:05,526 --> 00:20:06,526
for the most part,

425
00:20:06,526 --> 00:20:09,526
hasn't worked that way.

426
00:20:09,526 --> 00:20:12,526
You have to allow for the possibility.

427
00:20:12,526 --> 00:20:13,526
The possibility is that,

428
00:20:13,526 --> 00:20:18,526
that political philosophy may make you a worse citizen,

429
00:20:18,526 --> 00:20:21,526
rather than a better one,

430
00:20:21,526 --> 00:20:23,526
or at least a worse citizen

431
00:20:23,526 --> 00:20:27,526
before it makes you a better one.

432
00:20:27,526 --> 00:20:31,526
And that's because philosophy is a distancing,

433
00:20:31,526 --> 00:20:35,526
even debilitating activity.

434
00:20:35,526 --> 00:20:39,526
And you see this going back to Socrates.

435
00:20:39,526 --> 00:20:41,526
There's a dialogue, the Gorgias,

436
00:20:41,526 --> 00:20:43,526
in which one of Socrates' friends,

437
00:20:43,526 --> 00:20:44,526
Callicles,

438
00:20:44,526 --> 00:20:48,526
tries to talk him out of philosophizing.

439
00:20:48,526 --> 00:20:50,526
Callicles tells Socrates,

440
00:20:50,526 --> 00:20:53,526
philosophy is a pretty toy

441
00:20:53,526 --> 00:20:55,526
if one indulges in it with moderation

442
00:20:55,526 --> 00:20:57,526
at the right time of life.

443
00:20:57,526 --> 00:20:59,526
But if one pursues it further than one should,

444
00:20:59,526 --> 00:21:02,526
it is absolute ruin.

445
00:21:02,526 --> 00:21:05,526
Take my advice, Callicles says.

446
00:21:05,526 --> 00:21:07,526
Abandon argument.

447
00:21:07,526 --> 00:21:10,526
Learn the accomplishments of active life.

448
00:21:10,526 --> 00:21:12,526
Take for your models not those people

449
00:21:12,526 --> 00:21:15,526
who spend their time on these petty quibbles,

450
00:21:15,526 --> 00:21:17,526
but those who have a good livelihood

451
00:21:17,526 --> 00:21:21,526
and reputation and many other blessings.

452
00:21:21,526 --> 00:21:25,526
So Callicles is really saying to Socrates,

453
00:21:25,526 --> 00:21:29,526
quit philosophizing, get real,

454
00:21:29,526 --> 00:21:31,526
go to business school.

455
00:21:34,526 --> 00:21:37,526
And Callicles did have a point.

456
00:21:37,526 --> 00:21:41,526
He had a point because philosophy distances us

457
00:21:41,526 --> 00:21:44,526
from conventions, from established assumptions,

458
00:21:44,526 --> 00:21:46,526
and from settled beliefs.

459
00:21:46,526 --> 00:21:49,526
Those are the risks, personal and political.

460
00:21:49,526 --> 00:21:51,526
And in the face of these risks,

461
00:21:51,526 --> 00:21:53,526
there is a characteristic evasion.

462
00:21:53,526 --> 00:21:56,526
The name of the evasion is skepticism.

463
00:21:56,526 --> 00:21:58,526
It's the idea, well, it goes something like this.

464
00:21:58,526 --> 00:22:02,526
We didn't resolve once and for all

465
00:22:02,526 --> 00:22:05,526
either the cases or the principles

466
00:22:05,526 --> 00:22:09,526
we were arguing when we began.

467
00:22:09,526 --> 00:22:10,526
And if Aristotle,

468
00:22:10,526 --> 00:22:12,526
and Locke, and Kant, and Mill

469
00:22:12,526 --> 00:22:13,526
haven't solved these questions

470
00:22:13,526 --> 00:22:16,526
after all of these years,

471
00:22:16,526 --> 00:22:18,526
who are we to think

472
00:22:18,526 --> 00:22:20,526
that we here in Sanders Theatre

473
00:22:20,526 --> 00:22:22,526
over the course of a semester

474
00:22:22,526 --> 00:22:25,526
can resolve them?

475
00:22:25,526 --> 00:22:28,526
And so maybe it's just a matter of

476
00:22:28,526 --> 00:22:31,526
each person having his or her own principles

477
00:22:31,526 --> 00:22:33,526
and there's nothing more to be said about it.

478
00:22:33,526 --> 00:22:35,526
No way of reasoning.

479
00:22:35,526 --> 00:22:38,526
That's the evasion, the evasion of skepticism,

480
00:22:38,526 --> 00:22:40,526
to which I would offer the following.

481
00:22:40,526 --> 00:22:44,526
It's true, these questions have been debated

482
00:22:44,526 --> 00:22:46,526
for a very long time.

483
00:22:46,526 --> 00:22:49,526
But the very fact that they have recurred

484
00:22:49,526 --> 00:22:53,526
and persisted may suggest

485
00:22:53,526 --> 00:22:56,526
that though they're impossible in one sense,

486
00:22:56,526 --> 00:22:58,526
they're unavoidable in another.

487
00:22:58,526 --> 00:23:01,526
And the reason they're unavoidable,

488
00:23:01,526 --> 00:23:03,526
the reason they're inescapable,

489
00:23:03,526 --> 00:23:05,526
is that we live some answer

490
00:23:05,526 --> 00:23:08,526
to these questions every day.

491
00:23:08,526 --> 00:23:10,526
So skepticism is the answer

492
00:23:10,526 --> 00:23:12,526
to the question of

493
00:23:12,526 --> 00:23:15,526
what is the answer to the question of skepticism?

494
00:23:15,526 --> 00:23:17,526
And the answer to that question

495
00:23:17,526 --> 00:23:19,526
is that skepticism,

496
00:23:19,526 --> 00:23:21,526
just throwing up your hands

497
00:23:21,526 --> 00:23:24,526
and giving up on moral reflection,

498
00:23:24,526 --> 00:23:26,526
is no solution.

499
00:23:26,526 --> 00:23:28,526
Immanuel Kant described very well

500
00:23:28,526 --> 00:23:30,526
the problem with skepticism when he wrote,

501
00:23:30,526 --> 00:23:33,526
skepticism is a resting place for human reason,

502
00:23:33,526 --> 00:23:36,526
where it can reflect upon its dogmatic wanderings,

503
00:23:36,526 --> 00:23:39,526
but it is no dwelling place for permanent settlement.

504
00:23:40,526 --> 00:23:43,526
I've tried to suggest,

505
00:23:43,526 --> 00:23:45,526
through these stories and these arguments,

506
00:23:45,526 --> 00:23:49,526
some sense of the risks and temptations

507
00:23:49,526 --> 00:23:51,526
of the perils and the possibilities.

508
00:23:51,526 --> 00:23:54,526
I would simply conclude by saying

509
00:23:54,526 --> 00:23:57,526
that the aim of this course

510
00:23:57,526 --> 00:24:01,526
is to awaken the restlessness of reason

511
00:24:01,526 --> 00:24:03,526
and to see where it might lead.

512
00:24:03,526 --> 00:24:05,526
Thank you very much.

513
00:24:10,526 --> 00:24:12,526
Thank you.

514
00:24:40,526 --> 00:24:44,526
And last time,

515
00:24:44,526 --> 00:24:46,526
we started out last time

516
00:24:46,526 --> 00:24:48,526
with some stories,

517
00:24:48,526 --> 00:24:50,526
with some moral dilemmas

518
00:24:50,526 --> 00:24:52,526
about trolley cars

519
00:24:52,526 --> 00:24:54,526
and about doctors

520
00:24:54,526 --> 00:24:56,526
and healthy patients

521
00:24:56,526 --> 00:24:58,526
vulnerable to being victims

522
00:24:58,526 --> 00:25:00,526
of organ transplantation.

523
00:25:00,526 --> 00:25:03,526
We noticed two things

524
00:25:03,526 --> 00:25:06,526
about the arguments we had.

525
00:25:06,526 --> 00:25:09,526
One had to do with the way we were arguing.

526
00:25:09,526 --> 00:25:13,526
We began with our judgments in particular cases.

527
00:25:13,526 --> 00:25:15,526
We tried to articulate the reasons

528
00:25:15,526 --> 00:25:17,526
or the principles

529
00:25:17,526 --> 00:25:21,526
lying behind our judgments.

530
00:25:21,526 --> 00:25:24,526
And then, confronted with a new case,

531
00:25:24,526 --> 00:25:29,526
we found ourselves reexamining those principles,

532
00:25:29,526 --> 00:25:33,526
revising each in the light of the other.

533
00:25:33,526 --> 00:25:35,526
And we noticed the built-in pressure

534
00:25:35,526 --> 00:25:37,526
to try to bring into alignment

535
00:25:37,526 --> 00:25:39,526
our judgments about the reasons,

536
00:25:39,526 --> 00:25:41,526
about particular cases,

537
00:25:41,526 --> 00:25:43,526
and the principles we would endorse

538
00:25:43,526 --> 00:25:45,526
on reflection.

539
00:25:45,526 --> 00:25:47,526
We also noticed something

540
00:25:47,526 --> 00:25:49,526
about the substance of the arguments

541
00:25:49,526 --> 00:25:52,526
that emerged from the discussion.

542
00:25:54,526 --> 00:25:56,526
We noticed that sometimes

543
00:25:56,526 --> 00:25:58,526
we were tempted to locate the morality

544
00:25:58,526 --> 00:26:00,526
of an act and the consequences

545
00:26:00,526 --> 00:26:01,526
in the results,

546
00:26:01,526 --> 00:26:02,526
in the state of the world

547
00:26:02,526 --> 00:26:04,526
that it brought about.

548
00:26:05,526 --> 00:26:09,526
And we called this consequentialist moral reasoning.

549
00:26:09,526 --> 00:26:14,526
But we also noticed that in some cases

550
00:26:14,526 --> 00:26:19,526
we weren't swayed only by the result.

551
00:26:19,526 --> 00:26:24,526
Sometimes, many of us felt,

552
00:26:24,526 --> 00:26:26,526
that not just consequences

553
00:26:26,526 --> 00:26:28,526
but also the intrinsic quality

554
00:26:28,526 --> 00:26:30,526
or character of the act

555
00:26:30,526 --> 00:26:33,526
matters morally.

556
00:26:33,526 --> 00:26:37,526
Some people argued that there are certain things

557
00:26:37,526 --> 00:26:40,526
that are just categorically wrong

558
00:26:40,526 --> 00:26:43,526
even if they bring about a good result,

559
00:26:43,526 --> 00:26:46,526
even if they save five people

560
00:26:46,526 --> 00:26:49,526
at the cost of one life.

561
00:26:49,526 --> 00:26:53,526
So we contrasted consequentialist moral principles

562
00:26:53,526 --> 00:26:56,526
with categorical ones.

563
00:26:57,526 --> 00:27:00,526
Today, and in the next few days,

564
00:27:00,526 --> 00:27:02,526
we will begin to examine

565
00:27:02,526 --> 00:27:05,526
one of the most influential versions

566
00:27:05,526 --> 00:27:09,526
of consequentialist moral theory.

567
00:27:10,526 --> 00:27:13,526
And that's the philosophy of utilitarianism.

568
00:27:15,526 --> 00:27:18,526
Jeremy Bentham, the 18th century

569
00:27:18,526 --> 00:27:20,526
English political philosopher,

570
00:27:20,526 --> 00:27:25,526
gave first the first clear systematic expression

571
00:27:25,526 --> 00:27:29,526
to the utilitarian moral theory.

572
00:27:31,526 --> 00:27:33,526
And Bentham's idea

573
00:27:35,526 --> 00:27:37,526
his essential idea

574
00:27:37,526 --> 00:27:39,526
is a very simple one.

575
00:27:42,526 --> 00:27:47,526
With a lot of morally intuitive appeal.

576
00:27:47,526 --> 00:27:51,526
Bentham's idea is the following.

577
00:27:51,526 --> 00:27:53,526
The right thing to do,

578
00:27:53,526 --> 00:27:56,526
the just thing to do,

579
00:27:56,526 --> 00:28:01,526
is to maximize utility.

580
00:28:01,526 --> 00:28:03,526
What did he mean by utility?

581
00:28:05,526 --> 00:28:08,526
He meant by utility the balance

582
00:28:08,526 --> 00:28:12,526
of pleasure over pain,

583
00:28:12,526 --> 00:28:15,526
happiness over suffering.

584
00:28:15,526 --> 00:28:17,526
Here's how he arrived

585
00:28:17,526 --> 00:28:21,526
at the principle of maximizing utility.

586
00:28:21,526 --> 00:28:23,526
He started out by observing

587
00:28:23,526 --> 00:28:25,526
that all of us,

588
00:28:25,526 --> 00:28:27,526
all human beings,

589
00:28:27,526 --> 00:28:30,526
are governed by two sovereign masters.

590
00:28:30,526 --> 00:28:32,526
Pain and pleasure.

591
00:28:32,526 --> 00:28:34,526
We human beings,

592
00:28:34,526 --> 00:28:36,526
we human beings,

593
00:28:36,526 --> 00:28:39,526
like pleasure and dislike pain.

594
00:28:41,526 --> 00:28:44,526
And so we should base morality,

595
00:28:45,526 --> 00:28:46,526
whether we're thinking about

596
00:28:46,526 --> 00:28:48,526
what to do in our own lives,

597
00:28:48,526 --> 00:28:52,526
or whether as legislators or citizens,

598
00:28:52,526 --> 00:28:54,526
we're thinking about what the laws should be.

599
00:28:56,526 --> 00:28:58,526
The right thing to do,

600
00:28:58,526 --> 00:29:00,526
individually or collectively,

601
00:29:01,526 --> 00:29:03,526
is to maximize, act in a way,

602
00:29:03,526 --> 00:29:08,526
that maximizes the overall level of happiness.

603
00:29:10,526 --> 00:29:13,526
Bentham's utilitarianism is sometimes summed up

604
00:29:13,526 --> 00:29:14,526
with the slogan,

605
00:29:14,526 --> 00:29:17,526
the greatest good for the greatest number.

606
00:29:17,526 --> 00:29:22,526
With this basic principle of utility on hand,

607
00:29:22,526 --> 00:29:25,526
let's begin to test it and to examine it

608
00:29:25,526 --> 00:29:27,526
by turning to another case,

609
00:29:27,526 --> 00:29:29,526
another story, but this time,

610
00:29:29,526 --> 00:29:31,526
not a hypothetical story,

611
00:29:31,526 --> 00:29:32,526
a real life story,

612
00:29:32,526 --> 00:29:35,526
the case of the Queen versus Dudley and Stevens.

613
00:29:35,526 --> 00:29:38,526
This was a 19th century British law case

614
00:29:38,526 --> 00:29:42,526
that's famous and much debated in law schools.

615
00:29:43,526 --> 00:29:45,526
Here's what happened in the case.

616
00:29:46,526 --> 00:29:48,526
I'll summarize the story,

617
00:29:48,526 --> 00:29:51,526
then I want to hear how you would rule,

618
00:29:52,526 --> 00:29:54,526
imagining that you were the jury.

619
00:29:56,526 --> 00:29:59,526
The case of the Queen versus Dudley and Stevens.

620
00:29:59,526 --> 00:30:05,526
A newspaper account of the time

621
00:30:05,526 --> 00:30:07,526
described the background.

622
00:30:07,526 --> 00:30:10,526
A sadder story of disaster at sea

623
00:30:10,526 --> 00:30:13,526
was never told than that of the survivors

624
00:30:13,526 --> 00:30:15,526
of the yacht Minionet.

625
00:30:15,526 --> 00:30:17,526
The ship foundered in the South Atlantic,

626
00:30:17,526 --> 00:30:20,526
1300 miles from the Cape.

627
00:30:20,526 --> 00:30:22,526
There were four in the crew.

628
00:30:22,526 --> 00:30:24,526
Dudley was the captain.

629
00:30:24,526 --> 00:30:27,526
Stevens was the first mate.

630
00:30:27,526 --> 00:30:29,526
Brooks was a sailor.

631
00:30:29,526 --> 00:30:31,526
All men of excellent character,

632
00:30:31,526 --> 00:30:34,526
or so the newspaper account tells us.

633
00:30:34,526 --> 00:30:37,526
The fourth crew member was the cabin boy,

634
00:30:37,526 --> 00:30:40,526
Richard Parker, 17 years old.

635
00:30:42,526 --> 00:30:44,526
He was an orphan.

636
00:30:44,526 --> 00:30:46,526
He had no family.

637
00:30:46,526 --> 00:30:49,526
And he was on his first long voyage at sea.

638
00:30:50,526 --> 00:30:52,526
He went, the news account tells us,

639
00:30:52,526 --> 00:30:55,526
rather against the advice of his friends,

640
00:30:55,526 --> 00:30:57,526
he went in the hopeful mood,

641
00:30:57,526 --> 00:30:59,526
a world of youthful ambition,

642
00:30:59,526 --> 00:31:02,526
thinking the journey would make a man of him.

643
00:31:02,526 --> 00:31:04,526
Sadly, it was not to be.

644
00:31:04,526 --> 00:31:07,526
The facts of the case were not in dispute.

645
00:31:07,526 --> 00:31:08,526
A wave hit the ship,

646
00:31:08,526 --> 00:31:10,526
and the Minionet went down.

647
00:31:11,526 --> 00:31:14,526
The four crew members escaped to a lifeboat.

648
00:31:14,526 --> 00:31:17,526
The only food they had

649
00:31:17,526 --> 00:31:21,526
were two cans of preserved turnips.

650
00:31:21,526 --> 00:31:23,526
No fresh water.

651
00:31:23,526 --> 00:31:26,526
For the first three days, they ate nothing.

652
00:31:26,526 --> 00:31:27,526
On the fourth day,

653
00:31:27,526 --> 00:31:29,526
they opened one of the

654
00:31:29,526 --> 00:31:31,526
cans of turnips and ate it.

655
00:31:31,526 --> 00:31:33,526
The next day, they caught a turtle.

656
00:31:33,526 --> 00:31:36,526
Together with the other can of turnips,

657
00:31:36,526 --> 00:31:39,526
the turtle enabled them to subsist

658
00:31:39,526 --> 00:31:40,526
for the next few days.

659
00:31:40,526 --> 00:31:42,526
And then for eight days,

660
00:31:42,526 --> 00:31:43,526
they had nothing.

661
00:31:43,526 --> 00:31:45,526
No food, no water.

662
00:31:46,526 --> 00:31:49,526
Imagine yourself in a situation like that.

663
00:31:49,526 --> 00:31:51,526
What would you do?

664
00:31:52,526 --> 00:31:54,526
Here's what they did.

665
00:31:54,526 --> 00:31:56,526
By now, the cabin boy, Parker,

666
00:31:56,526 --> 00:31:58,526
is lying at the bottom of the lifeboat,

667
00:31:58,526 --> 00:32:00,526
in the corner,

668
00:32:00,526 --> 00:32:02,526
because he had drunk sea water

669
00:32:02,526 --> 00:32:04,526
against the advice of the others,

670
00:32:04,526 --> 00:32:06,526
and he had become ill,

671
00:32:06,526 --> 00:32:09,526
and he appeared to be dying.

672
00:32:09,526 --> 00:32:11,526
So on the 19th day,

673
00:32:11,526 --> 00:32:13,526
Dudley, the captain, suggested

674
00:32:13,526 --> 00:32:16,526
that they should all have a lottery,

675
00:32:16,526 --> 00:32:18,526
that they should draw lots

676
00:32:18,526 --> 00:32:20,526
to see who would die

677
00:32:20,526 --> 00:32:22,526
to save the rest.

678
00:32:23,526 --> 00:32:25,526
Brooks refused.

679
00:32:25,526 --> 00:32:28,526
He didn't like the lottery idea.

680
00:32:28,526 --> 00:32:31,526
We don't know whether this was because

681
00:32:31,526 --> 00:32:33,526
he didn't want to take the chance

682
00:32:33,526 --> 00:32:36,526
or because he believed in categorical moral principles.

683
00:32:36,526 --> 00:32:38,526
But in any case,

684
00:32:38,526 --> 00:32:40,526
no lots were drawn.

685
00:32:41,526 --> 00:32:42,526
The next day,

686
00:32:42,526 --> 00:32:44,526
there was still no ship in sight,

687
00:32:44,526 --> 00:32:47,526
so Dudley told Brooks to avert his gaze,

688
00:32:47,526 --> 00:32:49,526
and he motioned to Stevens

689
00:32:49,526 --> 00:32:52,526
that the boy, Parker, had better be killed.

690
00:32:53,526 --> 00:32:54,526
Dudley offered a prayer,

691
00:32:55,526 --> 00:32:57,526
and he told the boy his time had come,

692
00:32:57,526 --> 00:32:59,526
and he killed him with a penknife,

693
00:32:59,526 --> 00:33:02,526
stabbing him in the jugular vein.

694
00:33:03,526 --> 00:33:06,526
Brooks emerged from his conscientious objection

695
00:33:06,526 --> 00:33:08,526
to share in the gruesome bounty.

696
00:33:09,526 --> 00:33:10,526
For four days,

697
00:33:10,526 --> 00:33:12,526
the three of them fed on the body and blood

698
00:33:12,526 --> 00:33:14,526
of the cabin boy.

699
00:33:14,526 --> 00:33:16,526
True story.

700
00:33:16,526 --> 00:33:18,526
And then they were rescued.

701
00:33:18,526 --> 00:33:21,526
Dudley describes their rescue

702
00:33:22,526 --> 00:33:23,526
in his diary,

703
00:33:23,526 --> 00:33:26,526
with staggering euphemism,

704
00:33:26,526 --> 00:33:27,526
quote,

705
00:33:27,526 --> 00:33:29,526
on the 24th day,

706
00:33:29,526 --> 00:33:31,526
as we were having our breakfast,

707
00:33:34,526 --> 00:33:37,526
a ship appeared at last.

708
00:33:38,526 --> 00:33:41,526
The three survivors were picked up by a German ship.

709
00:33:41,526 --> 00:33:43,526
They were taken back to Falmouth in England,

710
00:33:43,526 --> 00:33:46,526
where they were arrested and tried.

711
00:33:46,526 --> 00:33:49,526
Brooks turned state's witness.

712
00:33:49,526 --> 00:33:51,526
Dudley and Stevens went to trial.

713
00:33:51,526 --> 00:33:53,526
They didn't dispute the facts.

714
00:33:53,526 --> 00:33:56,526
They claimed they had acted out of necessity.

715
00:33:56,526 --> 00:33:58,526
That was their defense.

716
00:33:58,526 --> 00:34:00,526
They argued, in effect,

717
00:34:00,526 --> 00:34:02,526
better that one should die,

718
00:34:02,526 --> 00:34:05,526
so that three could survive.

719
00:34:05,526 --> 00:34:09,525
The prosecutor wasn't swayed by that argument.

720
00:34:09,525 --> 00:34:11,525
He said murder is murder,

721
00:34:11,525 --> 00:34:13,525
and so the case went to trial.

722
00:34:13,525 --> 00:34:15,525
Now imagine you are the jury.

723
00:34:15,525 --> 00:34:17,525
And just to simplify the discussion,

724
00:34:17,525 --> 00:34:20,525
put aside the question of law,

725
00:34:20,525 --> 00:34:22,525
and let's assume that you as the jury,

726
00:34:22,525 --> 00:34:25,525
are charged with deciding

727
00:34:25,525 --> 00:34:29,525
whether what they did was morally permissible or not.

728
00:34:29,525 --> 00:34:36,525
How many would vote not guilty

729
00:34:36,525 --> 00:34:42,525
that what they did was morally permissible?

730
00:34:46,525 --> 00:34:48,525
And how many would vote guilty

731
00:34:48,525 --> 00:34:50,525
what they did was morally permissible?

732
00:34:50,525 --> 00:34:52,525
How many would vote guilty

733
00:34:52,525 --> 00:34:54,525
what they did was morally wrong?

734
00:34:54,525 --> 00:34:57,526
A pretty sizable majority.

735
00:34:57,526 --> 00:35:00,526
Now let's see what people's reasons are,

736
00:35:00,526 --> 00:35:02,026
and let me begin with

737
00:35:02,026 --> 00:35:03,526
those who are in the minority.

738
00:35:03,526 --> 00:35:07,526
Let's hear first from the defense

739
00:35:07,526 --> 00:35:09,526
of Dudley and Stevens.

740
00:35:09,526 --> 00:35:13,526
Why would you morally exonerate them?

741
00:35:13,526 --> 00:35:16,526
What are your reasons?

742
00:35:16,526 --> 00:35:17,526
Yes.

743
00:35:17,526 --> 00:35:19,526
I think it is morally reprehensible,

744
00:35:19,526 --> 00:35:21,526
but I think that there is a distinction

745
00:35:21,526 --> 00:35:23,526
between what's morally reprehensible

746
00:35:23,526 --> 00:35:25,526
and what makes someone legally accountable.

747
00:35:25,526 --> 00:35:27,526
In other words, you know, as the judge said,

748
00:35:27,526 --> 00:35:30,526
what's always moral isn't necessarily against the law.

749
00:35:30,526 --> 00:35:35,526
And while I don't think that necessity justifies theft

750
00:35:35,526 --> 00:35:37,526
or murder or any illegal act,

751
00:35:37,526 --> 00:35:40,526
at some point, your degree of necessity

752
00:35:40,526 --> 00:35:43,526
does in fact exonerate you from any guilt.

753
00:35:43,526 --> 00:35:44,526
Okay, good.

754
00:35:44,526 --> 00:35:48,526
Other defenders, other voices for the defense.

755
00:35:48,526 --> 00:35:53,526
Moral justifications for what they did.

756
00:35:53,526 --> 00:35:54,526
Yes.

757
00:35:54,526 --> 00:35:56,526
All right, thank you.

758
00:35:56,526 --> 00:36:00,526
I just feel like in a situation that desperate,

759
00:36:00,526 --> 00:36:01,526
you have to do what

760
00:36:01,526 --> 00:36:02,526
you have to do to survive.

761
00:36:02,526 --> 00:36:04,526
You have to do what you have to do.

762
00:36:04,526 --> 00:36:05,526
You have to do what

763
00:36:05,526 --> 00:36:06,526
you have to do, pretty much.

764
00:36:06,526 --> 00:36:09,526
If you've been going 19 days without any food,

765
00:36:09,526 --> 00:36:11,526
you know, someone just has to take the sacrifice.

766
00:36:11,526 --> 00:36:12,526
Someone has to make the sacrifice,

767
00:36:12,526 --> 00:36:13,526
and people can survive.

768
00:36:13,526 --> 00:36:16,526
And furthermore, from that, let's say they survive,

769
00:36:16,526 --> 00:36:18,526
and then they become productive members of the family,

770
00:36:18,526 --> 00:36:20,526
of the society who go home and start like

771
00:36:20,526 --> 00:36:22,526
a million charity organizations and this and that

772
00:36:22,526 --> 00:36:23,526
and this and that.

773
00:36:23,526 --> 00:36:25,526
I mean, they benefit everybody in the end.

774
00:36:25,526 --> 00:36:26,526
I mean, I don't know what they did afterwards.

775
00:36:26,526 --> 00:36:28,526
They might have gone and like, I don't know,

776
00:36:28,526 --> 00:36:30,526
killed more people, whatever.

777
00:36:30,526 --> 00:36:31,526
What?

778
00:36:31,526 --> 00:36:33,526
What if they went home and

779
00:36:33,526 --> 00:36:35,526
they turned out to be assassins?

780
00:36:35,526 --> 00:36:36,526
What if they went home

781
00:36:36,526 --> 00:36:37,526
and turned out to be assassins?

782
00:36:37,526 --> 00:36:38,526
Mm-hmm.

783
00:36:38,526 --> 00:36:41,526
You'd want to know who they assassinated.

784
00:36:41,526 --> 00:36:42,526
That's true, too.

785
00:36:42,526 --> 00:36:43,526
That's fair.

786
00:36:43,526 --> 00:36:44,526
That's fair.

787
00:36:44,526 --> 00:36:45,526
Okay.

788
00:36:45,526 --> 00:36:46,526
I want to know who they assassinated.

789
00:36:46,526 --> 00:36:47,526
All right, that's good.

790
00:36:47,526 --> 00:36:48,526
What's your name?

791
00:36:48,526 --> 00:36:49,526
Marcus.

792
00:36:49,526 --> 00:36:50,526
Marcus.

793
00:36:50,526 --> 00:36:51,526
All right.

794
00:36:51,526 --> 00:36:52,526
We've heard a defense, a

795
00:36:52,526 --> 00:36:53,526
couple of voices for the defense.

796
00:36:53,526 --> 00:36:56,526
Now we need to hear from the prosecution.

797
00:36:56,526 --> 00:37:00,526
Most people think what they did was wrong.

798
00:37:00,526 --> 00:37:02,526
Why?

799
00:37:02,526 --> 00:37:04,526
Yes.

800
00:37:04,526 --> 00:37:06,526
One of the first things that I was thinking was,

801
00:37:06,526 --> 00:37:07,526
oh, if they haven't been

802
00:37:07,526 --> 00:37:08,526
eating for a really long time,

803
00:37:08,526 --> 00:37:14,526
maybe they're mentally affected,

804
00:37:14,526 --> 00:37:17,526
and so then that could be used as a defense,

805
00:37:17,526 --> 00:37:18,526
a possible argument.

806
00:37:18,526 --> 00:37:19,526
But if they're not,

807
00:37:19,526 --> 00:37:20,526
then that's a good argument that,

808
00:37:20,526 --> 00:37:21,526
oh, they weren't in the proper state of mind.

809
00:37:21,526 --> 00:37:22,026
They weren't making decisions

810
00:37:22,026 --> 00:37:22,526
they might otherwise be making.

811
00:37:22,526 --> 00:37:23,526
And if that's an appealing argument,

812
00:37:23,526 --> 00:37:24,026
that you have to be in an

813
00:37:24,026 --> 00:37:24,526
altered mindset to do something like that,

814
00:37:24,526 --> 00:37:25,526
it suggests that people who find that argument convincing

815
00:37:25,526 --> 00:37:26,526
do think that they were acting immorally.

816
00:37:26,526 --> 00:37:27,526
But what do you,

817
00:37:27,526 --> 00:37:28,526
I want to know what you think.

818
00:37:28,526 --> 00:37:29,526
You defend them.

819
00:37:29,526 --> 00:37:30,526
Yeah.

820
00:37:30,526 --> 00:37:31,526
No, no, no.

821
00:37:31,526 --> 00:37:32,526
I'm sorry.

822
00:37:32,526 --> 00:37:33,526
You vote to convict, right?

823
00:37:33,526 --> 00:37:34,526
Yeah.

824
00:37:34,526 --> 00:37:35,026
I don't think that they

825
00:37:35,026 --> 00:37:35,526
acted in a morally appropriate way.

826
00:37:35,526 --> 00:37:36,526
And why not?

827
00:37:36,526 --> 00:37:37,526
What do you say?

828
00:37:37,526 --> 00:37:38,526
Here's the thing.

829
00:37:38,526 --> 00:37:39,526
I'm not saying that they were acting immorally.

830
00:37:39,526 --> 00:37:40,526
I'm not saying that they were acting immorally.

831
00:37:40,526 --> 00:37:41,526
I'm sorry.

832
00:37:41,526 --> 00:37:42,526
You vote to convict, right?

833
00:37:42,526 --> 00:37:43,526
Yeah.

834
00:37:43,526 --> 00:37:45,026
I don't think that they

835
00:37:45,026 --> 00:37:46,526
acted in a morally appropriate way.

836
00:37:46,526 --> 00:37:47,526
And why not?

837
00:37:47,526 --> 00:37:48,526
