00:47:41,586 --> 00:47:43,166
Like in Dudley's diary,

1111
00:47:43,166 --> 00:47:44,785
we were eating our breakfast.

1112
00:47:44,785 --> 00:47:46,405
It seems as though he's just sort of like,

1113
00:47:46,405 --> 00:47:48,825
um, you know, the whole idea

1114
00:47:48,825 --> 00:47:51,246
of not valuing someone else's life.

1115
00:47:51,246 --> 00:47:53,246
So that makes me feel like

1116
00:47:53,246 --> 00:47:55,246
I have to take the categorical stand.

1117
00:47:55,246 --> 00:47:57,365
You want to throw the book at him.

1118
00:47:57,365 --> 00:47:59,055
When he lacks remorse or a

1119
00:47:59,055 --> 00:48:00,746
sense of having done anything wrong.

1120
00:48:00,746 --> 00:48:02,006
Right.

1121
00:48:02,006 --> 00:48:03,646
So, all right, good.

1122
00:48:03,646 --> 00:48:08,646
Other, any other defenders of a, uh,

1123
00:48:08,646 --> 00:48:10,226
who say it's just categorically wrong,

1124
00:48:10,226 --> 00:48:11,366
with or without consent?

1125
00:48:11,366 --> 00:48:12,986
Yes, stand up.

1126
00:48:12,986 --> 00:48:13,526
Why?

1127
00:48:13,526 --> 00:48:15,686
I think undoubtedly to where our society is shaped,

1128
00:48:15,686 --> 00:48:16,966
murder is murder.

1129
00:48:16,966 --> 00:48:18,386
Murder is murder in every way.

1130
00:48:18,386 --> 00:48:19,886
And our society looks at murder

1131
00:48:19,886 --> 00:48:21,386
down on it in the same light.

1132
00:48:21,386 --> 00:48:23,006
And I don't think it's any different in any case.

1133
00:48:23,006 --> 00:48:23,206
Good.

1134
00:48:23,206 --> 00:48:24,566
Let me ask you a question.

1135
00:48:24,566 --> 00:48:26,246
There were three lives at stake.

1136
00:48:26,246 --> 00:48:26,886
Mm-hmm.

1137
00:48:26,886 --> 00:48:27,826
Versus one.

1138
00:48:27,826 --> 00:48:30,306
Okay.

1139
00:48:30,306 --> 00:48:31,766
The one, the cabin boy, he had to.

1140
00:48:31,766 --> 00:48:32,905
He had no family.

1141
00:48:32,905 --> 00:48:34,246
He had no dependents.

1142
00:48:34,246 --> 00:48:37,206
These other three had families back home in England.

1143
00:48:37,206 --> 00:48:38,626
They had dependents.

1144
00:48:38,626 --> 00:48:41,106
They had wives and children.

1145
00:48:41,106 --> 00:48:43,005
Think back to Bentham.

1146
00:48:43,005 --> 00:48:46,726
Bentham says we have to consider the welfare, the utility,

1147
00:48:46,726 --> 00:48:48,825
the happiness of everybody.

1148
00:48:48,825 --> 00:48:51,126
We have to add it all up.

1149
00:48:51,126 --> 00:48:54,425
So it's not just numbers three against one.

1150
00:48:54,425 --> 00:48:58,646
It's also all of those people at home.

1151
00:48:58,646 --> 00:49:00,726
In fact, the London newspaper at the time,

1152
00:49:00,726 --> 00:49:01,766
and popular opinion,

1153
00:49:01,766 --> 00:49:04,046
they didn't sympathize with them,

1154
00:49:04,046 --> 00:49:05,445
Dudley and Stephens.

1155
00:49:05,445 --> 00:49:09,586
And the paper said if they weren't motivated by affection

1156
00:49:09,586 --> 00:49:11,785
and concern for their loved ones at home

1157
00:49:11,785 --> 00:49:14,046
and their dependents, surely they wouldn't have done this.

1158
00:49:14,046 --> 00:49:15,345
Yeah, and how is that any different

1159
00:49:15,345 --> 00:49:17,626
from people on the corner trying to have

1160
00:49:17,626 --> 00:49:19,086
the same desire to feed their family?

1161
00:49:19,086 --> 00:49:20,026
I don't think it's any different.

1162
00:49:20,026 --> 00:49:22,445
I think in any case, if I'm murdering you

1163
00:49:22,445 --> 00:49:24,345
to advance my status, that's murder.

1164
00:49:24,345 --> 00:49:25,545
And I think that we should look

1165
00:49:25,545 --> 00:49:26,746
at that all in the same light.

1166
00:49:26,746 --> 00:49:29,905
Instead of criminalizing certain activities,

1167
00:49:29,905 --> 00:49:30,905
and making certain things seem like they're not, you know,

1168
00:49:30,906 --> 00:49:33,646
and making certain things seem more violent and savage,

1169
00:49:33,646 --> 00:49:35,706
when in the same case, it's all the same.

1170
00:49:35,706 --> 00:49:36,966
It's all the same act

1171
00:49:36,966 --> 00:49:38,226
and mentality that goes into murder,

1172
00:49:38,226 --> 00:49:39,846
necessity to feed your family, so.

1173
00:49:39,846 --> 00:49:42,866
Suppose it weren't three, suppose it were 30.

1174
00:49:44,466 --> 00:49:46,166
One life to save 300.

1175
00:49:47,286 --> 00:49:48,226
We're in war time.

1176
00:49:49,266 --> 00:49:51,026
Suppose the stakes are even bigger.

1177
00:49:51,026 --> 00:49:52,806
Suppose the stakes are even bigger.

1178
00:49:52,806 --> 00:49:54,226
I think it's still the same deal.

1179
00:49:54,226 --> 00:49:56,056
Do you think Bentham is wrong

1180
00:49:56,056 --> 00:49:57,886
to say the right thing to do

1181
00:49:57,886 --> 00:50:00,786
is to add up the collective happiness?

1182
00:50:00,906 --> 00:50:02,546
Do you think he's wrong about that?

1183
00:50:02,546 --> 00:50:03,386
I don't think he's wrong,

1184
00:50:03,386 --> 00:50:05,266
but I think murder is murder in any case.

1185
00:50:05,266 --> 00:50:06,786
Well, then Bentham has to be wrong.

1186
00:50:06,786 --> 00:50:08,266
If you're right, he's wrong.

1187
00:50:08,266 --> 00:50:09,186
Okay, then he's wrong.

1188
00:50:09,186 --> 00:50:10,006
All right.

1189
00:50:10,006 --> 00:50:10,846
I'm right.

1190
00:50:10,846 --> 00:50:12,866
Thank you, well done.

1191
00:50:12,866 --> 00:50:16,086
All right, let's step back from this discussion,

1192
00:50:16,086 --> 00:50:19,626
and notice how many objections have we heard

1193
00:50:21,726 --> 00:50:23,346
to what they did.

1194
00:50:23,346 --> 00:50:26,106
We heard some defenses of what they did.

1195
00:50:26,106 --> 00:50:28,966
The defenses had to do with necessity,

1196
00:50:28,966 --> 00:50:30,326
their dire circumstance,

1197
00:50:30,326 --> 00:50:34,486
and implicitly at least, the idea that numbers matter.

1198
00:50:34,486 --> 00:50:38,646
And not only numbers matter, but the wider effects matter.

1199
00:50:40,366 --> 00:50:43,326
Their families back home, their dependents.

1200
00:50:43,326 --> 00:50:44,806
Parker was an orphan.

1201
00:50:44,806 --> 00:50:46,666
No one would miss him.

1202
00:50:48,006 --> 00:50:51,726
So if you add up, if you try to calculate

1203
00:50:51,726 --> 00:50:55,046
the balance of happiness and suffering,

1204
00:50:55,046 --> 00:50:57,806
you might have a case for saying

1205
00:50:57,806 --> 00:50:59,846
what they did was the right thing.

1206
00:51:00,326 --> 00:51:02,406
And then we heard at

1207
00:51:02,406 --> 00:51:04,486
least three different types of objections.

1208
00:51:04,486 --> 00:51:07,486
We heard an objection that said,

1209
00:51:07,486 --> 00:51:09,886
what they did was categorically wrong.

1210
00:51:09,886 --> 00:51:11,366
Mike here at the end.

1211
00:51:11,366 --> 00:51:13,086
Categorically wrong.

1212
00:51:13,086 --> 00:51:15,286
Murder is murder, it's always wrong,

1213
00:51:15,286 --> 00:51:19,446
even if it increases the overall happiness of society.

1214
00:51:20,686 --> 00:51:22,446
A categorical objection.

1215
00:51:23,646 --> 00:51:25,366
But we still need to investigate

1216
00:51:25,366 --> 00:51:28,366
what they did was categorically wrong.

1217
00:51:28,366 --> 00:51:29,366
And that's what we're going to do.

1218
00:51:29,366 --> 00:51:31,446
We're going to investigate

1219
00:51:31,446 --> 00:51:33,526
why murder is categorically wrong.

1220
00:51:33,526 --> 00:51:37,026
Is it because even cabin

1221
00:51:37,026 --> 00:51:40,526
boys have certain fundamental rights?

1222
00:51:40,526 --> 00:51:43,026
And if that's the reason,

1223
00:51:43,026 --> 00:51:45,526
where do those rights come from,

1224
00:51:45,526 --> 00:51:48,526
if not from some idea of the larger welfare,

1225
00:51:48,526 --> 00:51:50,526
or utility, or happiness?

1226
00:51:50,526 --> 00:51:52,526
Question number one.

1227
00:51:52,526 --> 00:51:57,526
Others said a lottery would make a difference.

1228
00:51:57,526 --> 00:51:58,566
A fair procedure.

1229
00:51:58,566 --> 00:52:00,566
A fair procedure, Matt said.

1230
00:52:03,966 --> 00:52:07,966
But, and some people were swayed by that.

1231
00:52:07,966 --> 00:52:11,966
That's not a categorical objection exactly.

1232
00:52:11,966 --> 00:52:15,966
It's saying everybody has to be counted as an equal,

1233
00:52:15,966 --> 00:52:17,966
even though at the end of the day,

1234
00:52:17,966 --> 00:52:21,966
one can be sacrificed for the general welfare.

1235
00:52:21,966 --> 00:52:25,966
That leaves us with another question to investigate.

1236
00:52:25,966 --> 00:52:27,966
Why does agreement to a certain procedure,

1237
00:52:28,566 --> 00:52:33,566
even a fair procedure, justify whatever result flows

1238
00:52:33,566 --> 00:52:36,566
from the operation of that procedure?

1239
00:52:36,566 --> 00:52:38,566
Question number two.

1240
00:52:38,566 --> 00:52:43,566
And question number three, the basic idea of consent.

1241
00:52:43,566 --> 00:52:46,566
Kathleen got us onto this.

1242
00:52:46,566 --> 00:52:50,566
If the cabin boy had agreed himself,

1243
00:52:50,566 --> 00:52:55,566
and not under duress, as was added,

1244
00:52:55,566 --> 00:52:57,966
then it would be all right to take his life.

1245
00:52:57,966 --> 00:52:59,466
He would have had to take

1246
00:52:59,466 --> 00:53:00,966
his life to save the rest.

1247
00:53:00,966 --> 00:53:04,966
And even more people signed onto that idea.

1248
00:53:04,966 --> 00:53:08,966
But that raises a third philosophical question.

1249
00:53:08,966 --> 00:53:13,966
What is the moral work that consent does?

1250
00:53:13,966 --> 00:53:16,466
Why does an act of

1251
00:53:16,466 --> 00:53:18,966
consent make such a moral difference

1252
00:53:18,966 --> 00:53:20,966
that an act that would be

1253
00:53:20,966 --> 00:53:22,966
wrong, taking a life without consent,

1254
00:53:22,966 --> 00:53:26,966
is morally permissible with consent?

1255
00:53:27,966 --> 00:53:30,966
To investigate those three questions,

1256
00:53:30,966 --> 00:53:32,966
we're going to have to read some philosophers.

1257
00:53:32,966 --> 00:53:35,966
And starting next time, we're going to read

1258
00:53:35,966 --> 00:53:39,966
Bentham and John Stuart Mill, utilitarian philosophers.

